  <exercise>
    <title>Problem 1</title>

    <statement>
      <p>The trace of a square matrix <m>A</m> (denoted by <m>\text{tr}A</m> ) is the sum
            of the main diagonal entries.
            Show that if <m>A</m> is diagonalizable then the trace of <m>A</m> is the sum of
            all eigenvalues (counting repeated ones as many times as their multiplicities).
            You can do this by proving first that for any
            <m>A, B \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}</m>, we have
            <md>
                \
             \text{tr}(AB) = \text{tr}(BA).\
            </md>
            Then show that if <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are similar,
            <m>\text{tr}(A) = \text{tr}(B)</m>.</p>
</statement>

    <workspace/>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <title>Problem 2</title>

    <statement>
      <p>Find the eigenvalues (together with their multiplicities) and the corresponding
            eigenvectors of
            <md>
                \
             B = \begin{bmatrix}\
             1 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\\
             1 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\\
             0 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 &amp; 3 \\\
             0 &amp; 0 &amp; 3 &amp; 2\
            \end{bmatrix}.\
            </md> </p>
</statement>

    <workspace/>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <title>Problem 3</title>

    <statement>
      <p>Consider the following matrices:
            <md>
                \
             A = \begin{bmatrix}\
             4 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\\
             0 &amp; 4 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\\
             0 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 &amp; 0 \\\
             0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 2\
            \end{bmatrix}, \quad\
             B = \begin{bmatrix}\
             4 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\\
             1 &amp; 4 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\\
             0 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 &amp; 0 \\\
             0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 2\
            \end{bmatrix}, \quad\
             C = \begin{bmatrix}\
             4 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 4 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 &amp; 0 \\ 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 2\
            \end{bmatrix}.
            </md>
            Which are similar and which are not?
             </p>
    </statement>

    <workspace/>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <title>Problem 4</title>

    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>A</m> be diagonalizable, i.e., there is an invertible matrix <m>P</m> and
            a diagonal matrix
            <md>
                \
             D = \begin{bmatrix}d_{1}&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;0 \\ 0&amp;d_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;0 \\ \vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots \\ 0&amp;0&amp;\cdots&amp;d_{n}\end{bmatrix}
            </md>
            such that <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>.
            Find <m>e^{A}</m> in terms of <m>P</m> and the entries of <m>D</m>.
            (Recall that <m>e^{x} = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^{n}}{n!}</m>, and you can
            assume that infinite sums work with matrices the same way as finite ones.)
               </p>
    </statement>

    <workspace/>
  </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Problem 5</title>
    
        <statement>
        <p>
             Let <m>A, B \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}</m> with non-zero eigenvalues.
                           Show that <m>AB</m> and <m>BA</m> have the same eigenvalues together with their
                           multiplicities.
                                           </p>
        </statement>
    
        <workspace/>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>